                (AABW). A cold, dense water mass formed near Antarctica that drives the global deep ocean circulation.
              Basal melting
                Melting that occurs at the base of an ice shelf as a result of contact with ocean water.
              Circumpolar deep water
                (CDW). A relatively warm, salty water mass that can intrude onto the Antarctic continental shelf and melt ice shelves from below.
              Geothermal heat flux
                The transfer of heat from bedrock to the base of the grounded ice sheet, where it softens and/or melts the ice.
              Glacial isostatic adjustment
                (GIA). The solid Earth’s response to past changes in ice–ocean surface loading.
              Grounding line
                The boundary at which land ice transitions from resting on bedrock to floating on the ocean as an ice shelf.
              Hydrofracturing
                The process by which surface meltwater penetrates ice crevasses and potentially leads to ice-shelf collapse.
              Ice discharge
                The flow of ice from glaciers or ice sheets into the ocean, where it contributes to sea-level rise.
              Ice rises
                A well-defined, often dome-shaped, elevation of an otherwise flat ice shelf, which can rise several hundreds of metres above the surrounding ice surface. Ice rises act as pinning points that can affect the stability and dynamics of ice shelves and upstream ice.
              Ice shelf
                A floating extension of a land-based ice sheet, often buttressed by downstream pinning points.
              Ice-sheet collapse
                The rapid and sustained disintegration of a large ice sheet or ice-sheet sector, resulting in major ice loss and contributing to sea-level rise, often after a critical stability threshold has been crossed.
              Ice-sheet dynamics
                Changes in the flow of the ice sheet that are often driven by changes in buttressing or sliding.
              Ice-sheet mass balance
                The difference between snow accumulation and ice discharge into the ocean, which determines whether an ice sheet is growing or shrinking and hence reducing or increasing the global-mean sea level.
              Ice-Sheet Model Intercomparison Project
                (ISMIP6). A coordinated international effort to compare and improve ice-sheet models under various climate scenarios.
              Ice-shelf buttressing
                The support provided by an ice shelf that slows the flow of upstream grounded ice.
              Ice-shelf water
                Water with temperature below the surface freezing point that is produced by melt at depth, where increased pressure lowers the freezing point.
              Land-fast ice
                Sea ice that is attached to the coastline, grounded icebergs, ice shelves or shoals and remains relatively stationary with respect to the coast. Also termed fast ice.
              Marine ice-sheet instability
                (MISI) A feedback mechanism whereby retreat of a marine-based ice sheet on a retrograde slope leads to accelerated ice loss.
              Marine-grounded basin
                A region where the ice sheet rests on bedrock below sea level and hence has increased vulnerability to ocean-driven melt.
              Ocean heat flux
                The transfer of heat from the ocean to the base of ice shelves, where it influences basal melting.
              Ocean stratification
                The layering of ocean water, which largely results from temperature and salinity variations.
              Palaeoclimate
                Past climate conditions reconstructed using chemical or biological proxies that are present in ice cores, rocks and sediments; coring sites are selected on the basis of bathymetry and geophysical data.
              Projections
                Simulations of future climate conditions, including ice-sheet conditions, that are based on different greenhouse gas emission scenarios.
              Retrograde bed slope
                A bedrock slope that deepens inland, which can facilitate ocean water intrusion at the ice–bedrock interface that promotes MISI and unstable ice retreat.
              Sea-level equivalent
                The potential global mean sea-level rise if a given volume of ice were to melt completely.
              Subglacial basin
                A large topographic depression in the bedrock beneath an ice sheet or glacier that is filled by ice and commonly lies below sea level. Subglacial basins influence ice flow, ice-sheet stability, sediment accumulation and interactions among the ice sheet, ocean and solid Earth.
              Subglacial hydrology
                Water redistribution beneath glaciers and ice sheets, including lakes and drainage systems.
              Surface mass balance
                (SMB). The net gain or loss of ice or snow on the upper surface of a glacier or ice sheet, largely caused by precipitation (in Antarctica).
